From d3bc7e5a55c473977b518cd973aa8e05e1de1872 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 08:35:04 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: EPOLLOUT means dequeueable, not "has a free
 slot"

virtio_media_device_poll() raised EPOLLOUT whenever the OUTPUT queue
had a free slot:

    else if (output_queue->queued_bufs < output_queue->allocated_bufs)
            rc |= EPOLLOUT | EPOLLWRNORM;

That is not what EPOLLOUT means for a V4L2 streaming queue.
vb2_core_poll() reports it only when q->done_list is non-empty -- a
completed buffer waiting to be dequeued -- and v4l2_m2m_poll() builds
on that. The CAPTURE branch of this same function already had it right
(!list_empty(&pending_dqbufs)); only the OUTPUT branch was
inconsistent.

A free slot is almost always available, so poll() returned immediately
and any client that polls in a loop spun at full speed. Measured in
DomA on RPi4/AAOS17, 223,200 ioctls for about 720 decoded frames:

    POLL  = 413,718 (575 per frame, 69-77 us avg)
    DQBUF = 212,162 (295 per frame, 7 us avg -- all EAGAIN)

v4l2_codec2's V4L2DevicePoller::devicePollTask() polls with an infinite
timeout and is written to block; it was never given the chance.

After the fix, same workload:

    POLL  =  51,131 (891 us avg -- it actually blocks now)
    DQBUF =   4,495
    POLL + DQBUF: about 870 per frame -> about 77

Total CPU for hardware-decoded 360x640 playback (xl list Time, DomA +
DomD, median of three runs) drops from 201.0 % to 162.8 %: DomA 121.5
-> 85.8, DomD 80.7 -> 77.3, qemu's main thread 49.6 -> 45.2. Frame rate
and dropped frames are unchanged (16.6 -> 16.1 fps, 5 -> 6 of 720), so
the same work is being done with 19 % less CPU. The gap to software
decode narrows from +66 to +28 points.

The client loses nothing: it decides when to QBUF from its own
bookkeeping (freeBuffersCount()), never from poll, and it needs waking
only when a queued bitstream buffer has actually completed.

Verified: the user-space buffer tests pass with allow_userptr=1 (the
parameter defaults to false, and without it only the USERPTR test
fails; playback uses DMABUF), VTS EOSTest OK, Wi-Fi unaffected. Numbers
and method measured on the board, 2026-09-04.

Change-Id: I2fe0ea2060da0957239191e05f85f3d836fb45b6
Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 driver/virtio_media_driver.c | 29 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/driver/virtio_media_driver.c b/driver/virtio_media_driver.c
index c32883a..071396a 100644
--- a/driver/virtio_media_driver.c
+++ b/driver/virtio_media_driver.c
@@ -726,8 +726,33 @@ static __poll_t virtio_media_device_poll(struct file *file, poll_table *wait)
 	if (req_events & (EPOLLOUT | EPOLLWRNORM)) {
 		if (!output_queue->streaming)
 			rc |= EPOLLERR;
-		else if (output_queue->queued_bufs <
-			 output_queue->allocated_bufs)
+		/*
+		 * EPOLLOUT means "a buffer can be dequeued from the OUTPUT
+		 * queue", not "the queue has a free slot to QBUF into". That
+		 * is what vb2_core_poll() does (it only reports EPOLLOUT when
+		 * q->done_list is non-empty) and what v4l2_m2m_poll() relies
+		 * on, and it is what the CAPTURE branch above already does.
+		 *
+		 * Reporting readiness on free space instead made poll() return
+		 * immediately essentially always -- the OUTPUT queue almost
+		 * always has a slot -- so a client that polls in a loop spins
+		 * at full speed. Measured in DomA on RPi4 (2026-09-04), over
+		 * 223,200 ioctls for about 720 decoded frames:
+		 *
+		 *     POLL  = 413,718 (575 per frame, 69-77 us each)
+		 *     DQBUF = 212,162 (295 per frame, 7 us each, all EAGAIN)
+		 *
+		 * That spin is what the codec2 HAL's 38 % of a core (68 % of
+		 * it stime) was; the decoder's poll thread woke, posted a
+		 * service task, dequeued nothing, and polled again. Measured on
+		 * the board, 2026-09-04.
+		 *
+		 * The client is not deprived of anything: it QBUFs from its own
+		 * bookkeeping (freeBuffersCount()), never from poll, and it
+		 * needs to be woken only when a queued bitstream buffer has
+		 * actually completed.
+		 */
+		else if (!list_empty(&output_queue->pending_dqbufs))
 			rc |= EPOLLOUT | EPOLLWRNORM;
 	}
 	mutex_unlock(&session->queues_lock);
